4. Define the states every asynchronous operation must support
Do not implement a loading spinner as the entire error-handling strategy. Each network or processing operation should have a predictable state model.

Idle

No request currently running

Loading / Processing

Progress, disabled duplicate actions, cancellation if supported

Success

Show valid data

Failure

Explain and recover

Empty / Partial / Offline

Explicit alternative states; never pretend the request succeeded

For long-running scan jobs, add specific states:

created → uploading → extracting → awaiting_confirmation → queued → analyzing → validating → completed

Failure states should be explicit, for example upload_failed, ocr_failed, analysis_failed, evidence_unavailable or cancelled. A failure should not be represented as completed.

A timeout is also not proof that a request failed on the server: the operation may still be running. Use a stable scan/job ID and retrieve the existing status instead of blindly submitting a duplicate job.

5. Backend and API logic
The Android application must not directly access the production database or contain privileged AI-provider credentials. Use authenticated backend APIs for user data and analysis orchestration.

These are proposed endpoint contracts; adapt them to your existing implementation after auditing the repository.

Endpoint

Purpose

POST /v1/scans

Create a scan record and return a stable scan ID.

POST /v1/scans/{id}/images

Upload or register a validated image.

POST /v1/scans/{id}/extract

Request OCR extraction if it is performed server-side.

PUT /v1/scans/{id}/ingredients

Save the user-confirmed ingredient text.

POST /v1/scans/{id}/analyze

Queue analysis and return job/report status.

GET /v1/scans/{id}/status

Retrieve the current processing state.

GET /v1/scans/{id}/report

Retrieve the validated report.

GET /v1/scans

Retrieve the authenticated user's paginated history.

DELETE /v1/scans/{id}

Delete a scan subject to the documented retention policy.

GET /v1/ingredients/{id}

Retrieve canonical ingredient information and approved evidence.

PUT /v1/users/me/preferences

Save authenticated user preferences.

POST /v1/feedback

Submit a report or issue for review.

Example: submit an analysis job
The server should validate scan ownership, confirmed ingredient input and quota, then create or return the existing job for an idempotent request.

Illustrative response:

{
  "scan_id": "scn_example",
  "job_id": "job_example",
  "status": "queued",
  "status_url": "/v1/scans/scn_example/status"
}

The Android app then observes the status using polling with backoff or a supported event channel. When the job completes, it fetches the validated report.

Important application rules:

Every endpoint verifies the user's access to the requested resource.

Rate limits and quotas are enforced server-side.

Retries use idempotency where needed.

Images use private storage and validated uploads.

AI-generated text is validated against approved evidence and deterministic findings.

Missing evidence, OCR errors and model timeouts remain explicit failures or incomplete states.

The backend stores report versions, evidence references and applicable rule versions.

